[{"id":"30821","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"822","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Who was the Prime minister of India at the time of launching the first five year plan?</span></p>","option_1":"Jawaharlal Nehru","option_2":"Gulzarilal Nanda","option_3":"Lal Bahadur Shastri","option_4":"Indira Gandhi","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Jawaharlal Nehru.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The First Five Year Plan of India (1951-56), based on the Harrod-Domar model with a focus on agriculture, was launched during the tenure of Jawaharlal Nehru, India's first Prime Minister.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Nehru also chaired the Planning Commission that formulated it, reflecting his vision of state-led planned economic development.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30822","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"822","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>How many annual plans were launched between the seventh and eighth five year plan of India?</span></p>","option_1":"One","option_2":"Two","option_3":"Four","option_4":"Five","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Two.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Seventh Five Year Plan ended in 1990, but due to political instability, the Eighth Five Year Plan could not begin immediately; India instead had two Annual Plans, for 1990-91 and 1991-92.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Eighth Five Year Plan formally started only in 1992, after the economic reforms of 1991, making the intervening period one of annual planning rather than a five-year plan.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30823","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"822","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Who led the Mughal invasion of the Ahom kingdom in 1662?</span></p>","option_1":"Asad Khan","option_2":"Jafar Khan","option_3":"Sadullah Khan","option_4":"Mir Jumla","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Mir Jumla.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Mir Jumla II, the Mughal governor of Bengal under Aurangzeb, led a major invasion of the Ahom kingdom (Assam) in 1662, capturing the Ahom capital Garhgaon.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Although initially successful, the campaign faced heavy losses due to the region's difficult terrain, monsoon flooding, and guerrilla resistance, and the Mughals were eventually forced to make peace with the Ahoms (Treaty of Ghilajharighat, 1663).</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30824","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"822","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The soil is rich source of which of the following?</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>I. Nitrogen</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>II. Phosphorus</span></p>","option_1":"Only I","option_2":"Only II","option_3":"Both I and II","option_4":"Neither I nor II","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Both I and II.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Soil is a natural reservoir of essential plant nutrients, including macronutrients such as nitrogen and phosphorus, which are vital for plant growth, root development, and overall soil fertility.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Nitrogen is essential for protein and chlorophyll synthesis, while phosphorus is important for energy transfer and root development, making both statements I and II correct.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30825","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"822","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>'Dipavamsa' and 'Mahavamsa' are examples of which type of ancient literature?</span></p>","option_1":"Jain texts","option_2":"Buddhist texts","option_3":"Greek accounts","option_4":"Tamil Sangam poetry","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Buddhist texts.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Dipavamsa and Mahavamsa are ancient Pali chronicles composed in Sri Lanka, considered important Buddhist historical texts documenting the history of Buddhism and the island's rulers.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Mahavamsa in particular is a key source for the history of ancient India and Sri Lanka, including accounts of Emperor Ashoka's role in spreading Buddhism.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30826","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"822","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The conversion of glucose to pyruvate takes place in _____ of a cell.</span></p>","option_1":"nucleus","option_2":"plasma membrane","option_3":"cytoplasm","option_4":"lysosome","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - cytoplasm.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Glycolysis — the metabolic pathway that converts one molecule of glucose into two molecules of pyruvate — takes place in the cytoplasm (cytosol) of the cell, and does not require the presence of oxygen.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>This is distinct from the subsequent steps of cellular respiration (Krebs cycle and electron transport chain), which occur inside the mitochondria.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30827","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"822","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which of the following dance forms is known as the mother of all other classical dances?</span></p>","option_1":"Bihu","option_2":"Phag","option_3":"Mohiniyattam","option_4":"Bharatnatyam","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Bharatnatyam.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Bharatanatyam, originating in Tamil Nadu, is widely regarded as the mother of Indian classical dance forms, as it is one of the oldest classical dance styles and its foundational principles (as codified in the Natya Shastra) have influenced the development of several other classical dance forms of India.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>It traditionally combines Nritta (pure dance), Nritya (expressive dance), and Natya (dramatic elements) rooted in temple traditions.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30828","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"822","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which state is famous for the folk dance called 'Joodu Haligi'?</span></p>","option_1":"Chhattisgarh","option_2":"Karnataka","option_3":"Tripura","option_4":"Uttar Pradesh","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Karnataka.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Joodu Haligi is a traditional folk dance form associated with Karnataka, often performed by farmers during agricultural and harvest-related festivities.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Karnataka has a rich repertoire of folk dance traditions, including Yakshagana, Dollu Kunitha, and Veeragase, alongside Joodu Haligi.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30829","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"822","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Jaldapara National Park is in _____.</span></p>","option_1":"Bihar","option_2":"West Bengal","option_3":"Tamil Nadu","option_4":"Kerala","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - West Bengal.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Jaldapara National Park is located in the Alipurduar district of West Bengal, along the Torsa River in the Dooars region at the foothills of the Eastern Himalayas.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>It is well known for its population of the Indian one-horned rhinoceros, along with elephants, deer, and various bird species.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30830","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"822","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>'The Surajkund Crafts Mela' is held in which of the following Indian state?</span></p>","option_1":"Maharashtra","option_2":"Haryana","option_3":"Mizoram","option_4":"Tripura","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Haryana.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Surajkund International Crafts Mela is an annual fair held at Surajkund in Faridabad district, Haryana, showcasing handicrafts, handlooms, and cultural performances from across India and partner/theme countries.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>It is organised jointly by the Haryana Tourism department, Surajkund Mela Authority, and various central ministries, and is one of the largest crafts fairs in the world.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30831","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"822","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>'Kojagiri Purnima', primarily celebrated in Maharashtra, is observed in honour of which deity?</span></p>","option_1":"Lord Shiva","option_2":"Lord Ganesha","option_3":"Goddess Durga","option_4":"Goddess Lakshmi","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Goddess Lakshmi.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Kojagiri Purnima (also called Sharad Purnima), celebrated on the full moon night of the Hindu month of Ashwin, is primarily observed in Maharashtra in honour of Goddess Lakshmi, the goddess of wealth and prosperity.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>It is believed that the goddess visits homes on this night asking 'Ko jagarti?' ('Who is awake?'), and devotees stay up late, offer prayers, and consume a special milk-based drink (masala doodh) under the moonlight.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30832","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"822","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Who inspired the Khalsa with the belief that their destiny was to rule?</span></p>","option_1":"Guru Nanak","option_2":"Guru Gobind Singh","option_3":"Guru Har Krishan","option_4":"Guru Angad","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Guru Gobind Singh.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Guru Gobind Singh, the tenth Sikh Guru, founded the Khalsa Panth in 1699 and instilled in his followers a strong martial and spiritual identity, inspiring the belief that the Khalsa was destined to establish righteous rule (Raj Karega Khalsa).</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>This belief later played a significant role in shaping Sikh political consciousness, including the rise of Sikh Misls and eventually the Sikh Empire under Maharaja Ranjit Singh.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30833","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"822","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Weight of the object on the Moon = _____ × its weight on the Earth</span></p>","option_1":"1/2","option_2":"1/4","option_3":"1/6","option_4":"1/8","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - 1/6.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Moon's gravitational acceleration is approximately one-sixth (1/6th) that of the Earth's, because the Moon has significantly less mass and a smaller radius.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Since weight is directly proportional to the acceleration due to gravity at a location, the weight of an object on the Moon is about 1/6th of its weight on the Earth, even though its mass remains unchanged.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30834","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"822","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which of the following is an example of celestial body?</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>I. The Sun</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>II. The Moon</span></p>","option_1":"Only I","option_2":"Only II","option_3":"Both I and II","option_4":"Neither I nor II","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Both I and II.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>A celestial body (or astronomical object) refers to any naturally occurring physical entity in space, such as stars, planets, moons, asteroids, and comets.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Sun (a star) and the Moon (a natural satellite of the Earth) are both classified as celestial bodies, making both statements I and II correct.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30835","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"822","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which of the following was the first American astronaut, who set foot on moon?</span></p>","option_1":"Neil Armstrong","option_2":"Yuri Gagarin","option_3":"Kenneth D. Cameron","option_4":"Jerome Apt","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Neil Armstrong.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Neil Armstrong, commander of NASA's Apollo 11 mission, became the first person (and first American astronaut) to set foot on the Moon on July 20, 1969, famously stating, 'That's one small step for man, one giant leap for mankind.'</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Yuri Gagarin, in contrast, was a Soviet cosmonaut and the first human to journey into outer space (1961), but he did not walk on the Moon.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30836","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"822","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>'Dohe' songs of Chhattisgarh are sung during _____.</span></p>","option_1":"Weddings","option_2":"Diwali","option_3":"Funerals","option_4":"Child birth","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Diwali.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Dohe songs of Chhattisgarh are traditional folk verses/couplets sung during Diwali celebrations, often accompanying processions and folk performances such as Rawat Nacha, performed by the Yadav/Rawat community.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>These songs form an important part of Chhattisgarh's rich oral folk tradition associated with the festival.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30837","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"822","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Starting from a stationary position, a person paddles his bicycle to attain a velocity of 20 m/s in 10 seconds. Calculate the acceleration (in m/s²) of the bicycle.</span></p>","option_1":"0.75","option_2":"1.25","option_3":"2","option_4":"3.5","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - 2.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Acceleration = (Final velocity − Initial velocity) / Time taken = (20 − 0) / 10 = 2 m/s².</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Since the bicycle starts from rest (initial velocity = 0 m/s) and reaches 20 m/s in 10 seconds, applying the basic kinematic formula a = Δv/Δt gives an acceleration of 2 m/s².</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30838","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"822","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which of the following is a part of Mineral Security Partnership?</span></p>","option_1":"European Commission","option_2":"ASEAN","option_3":"African Union","option_4":"SAARC","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - European Commission.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Mineral Security Partnership (MSP), launched in 2022, is a collaboration of countries and organisations (including the USA, India, and others) aimed at securing critical mineral supply chains; the European Commission is one of its partner members.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The MSP focuses on catalysing investment in responsible critical mineral supply chains globally, particularly minerals needed for clean energy and advanced technologies.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30839","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"822","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which of the following institution is responsible for controlling the money supply in India?</span></p>","option_1":"National Bank for Rural Development","option_2":"Reserve Bank of India","option_3":"NITI Aayog","option_4":"Comptroller and Auditor General of India","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Reserve Bank of India.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Reserve Bank of India (RBI), established in 1935, is India's central bank and is responsible for formulating and implementing monetary policy, including controlling the money supply through tools like the repo rate, CRR, and SLR.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>NABARD focuses on rural/agricultural credit, NITI Aayog is a policy think tank, and the CAG audits government accounts — none of these regulate money supply.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30840","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"822","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>India is deficient in which of the following mineral?</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>I. Manganese ore</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>II. Chromite</span></p>","option_1":"Only I","option_2":"Only II","option_3":"Both I and II","option_4":"Neither I nor II","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Both I and II.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Despite having considerable reserves, India faces relative deficiency/shortfall issues in both manganese ore and chromite in terms of matching high-grade domestic demand, particularly for use in the steel and ferro-alloy industries.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>As per this question's key, both minerals (I and II) are considered examples of minerals in which India faces a deficiency.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30841","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"822","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Who is the author of the book 'Selection Day'?</span></p>","option_1":"Jeet Thayil","option_2":"Aravind Adiga","option_3":"Chetan Bhagat","option_4":"Amit Chaudhuri","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Aravind Adiga.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>'Selection Day' is a novel written by Aravind Adiga, the Booker Prize-winning author (for 'The White Tiger'), which explores the world of cricket, ambition, and family in Mumbai.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Adiga is known for his sharp social commentary on contemporary Indian society through his fiction.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30842","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"822","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Who is the author of the book 'Many Ramayanas, Many Lessons'?</span></p>","option_1":"Amish Tripathi","option_2":"Perumal Murugan","option_3":"Nikita Singh","option_4":"Anand Neelakantan","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Anand Neelakantan.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>'Many Ramayanas, Many Lessons' is authored by Anand Neelakantan, an Indian writer well known for his mythological retellings that offer alternative perspectives on epics like the Ramayana and Mahabharata.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Neelakantan is also known for works like 'Asura: Tale of the Vanquished' and 'Ajaya', which similarly reinterpret Indian mythology.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30843","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"822","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Kirti Stambh is located in which of the following city?</span></p>","option_1":"Chittorgarh","option_2":"Jaipur","option_3":"Jodhpur","option_4":"Bikaner","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Chittorgarh.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Kirti Stambh (Tower of Fame) is located within the Chittorgarh Fort in Rajasthan, built by a Jain merchant in the 12th century and dedicated to Adinatha, the first Jain Tirthankara.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>It stands alongside the more famous Vijay Stambh (Tower of Victory) within the same fort complex, and both are significant examples of medieval Rajasthani architecture.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30844","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"822","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Red Fort of Delhi was built by which of the following rulers?</span></p>","option_1":"Shah Jahan","option_2":"Akbar","option_3":"Babur","option_4":"Humayun","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Shah Jahan.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Red Fort (Lal Qila) in Delhi was built by the Mughal emperor Shah Jahan in 1648, as the fortified palace of his new capital, Shahjahanabad.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>It served as the main residence of Mughal emperors for nearly 200 years and is a UNESCO World Heritage Site, from where the Prime Minister of India delivers the Independence Day address.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30845","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"822","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which of the following metals is commonly used to coat cheaper metals in electroplating for ornaments?</span></p>","option_1":"Aluminum","option_2":"Iron","option_3":"Copper","option_4":"Silver or gold","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Silver or gold.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In electroplating for ornaments and jewellery, cheaper base metals are commonly coated with a thin layer of silver or gold through electrolysis, to give them an attractive, precious-metal-like appearance at a lower cost.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>This process involves passing an electric current through a solution containing silver or gold ions, causing them to deposit onto the surface of the base metal object.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""}]